        "Korea introduced a statutory minimum wage in 1988, initially for manufacturing establishments with ten or more employees, and extended it to all workplaces by 2000. A single national hourly rate applies to all industries, regions and ages, with limited exceptions for probationary workers in their first three months. The rate is set each summer for the following calendar year by the Minimum Wage Commission, a tripartite body of nine worker, nine employer and nine public-interest members, and is then announced by the Minister of Employment and Labor. Because the Commission's public-interest members are appointed by the government, the outcome reflects the government's policy stance to a considerable degree.",
        "Table 1 summarises the evolution of the minimum wage over 2014–2020. Between 2014 and 2017 the minimum rose by 7 to 8 percent a year in nominal terms, roughly two to three times the growth of the median wage. The decision of July 2017 broke with this pattern. The 2018 minimum of KRW 7,530 represented an increase of 16.4 percent, and the Kaitz index — the ratio of the minimum to the median hourly wage of full-time workers — jumped from 0.56 to 0.64. According to the Survey on Labour Conditions by Employment Type, the share of wage earners paid less than the new minimum in June 2017 was 18.2 percent, compared with 13.1 to 14.6 percent in the years before. The 2019 increase of 10.9 percent raised the Kaitz index further to 0.67, after which the government abandoned the KRW 10,000 target and the 2020 increase was limited to 2.9 percent.",
