        "The modern minimum-wage literature began with case studies comparing neighbouring jurisdictions. Card and Krueger {1} found no reduction in fast-food employment in New Jersey after its 1992 increase, and Dube, Lester and Reich {4} generalised the approach by comparing contiguous counties across state borders, finding small employment effects. Neumark and Wascher {2} challenged these results, arguing that the choice of control regions matters and that the bulk of the evidence points to negative effects on less-skilled workers. Meta-analyses by Doucouliagos and Stanley {21} and the review by Belman and Wolfson {20} conclude that average employment elasticities are small and that published estimates are affected by selection.",
