        "Consider a labour market in which firms differ in productivity and in their wage-setting power, and workers differ in skill. In a competitive segment, an increase in the minimum wage above the market wage of a worker raises the cost of employing her and reduces labour demand in proportion to the labour-demand elasticity and the size of the gap between the new minimum and her previous wage. In a monopsonistic segment, by contrast, an increase in the minimum up to the competitive wage can raise employment, because it removes the firm's incentive to restrict hiring to hold down wages [11]. The aggregate effect of a reform depends on the mix of these segments among bound workers and on how far above the previous wages the new minimum is set.",
