        "How large are our estimates relative to the literature? Our design identifies the effect of the excess of the 2018 increase over a typical year, about 8.7 percentage points for a worker at the old minimum and somewhat less for the average treated worker, whose required increase was smaller. Scaling the 2.6 percent proportional reduction in employment by the 11.4 percent increase in hourly wages that it accompanied yields an own-wage employment elasticity of about −0.23. This is larger in absolute value than the near-zero estimates of Cengiz et al. {3} for U.S. state-level increases, but much smaller than the elasticity near −1 that a competitive model with standard labour-demand parameters would imply, and similar to estimates for large increases in Hungary [5] and for bound workers in the United Kingdom [17]. The reform therefore appears to have been binding — it destroyed a measurable number of jobs — but not extreme.",
        "The distribution of the effects matters as much as their average. On average across treated workers, the reform raised expected earnings by about 6.9 percent, a substantial gain for workers whose monthly earnings were around KRW 1.4 million. But the gains and losses were unequally distributed. The roughly 2 percent of treated workers who lost insured employment as a result of the reform, disproportionately older workers in small firms and elementary occupations, experienced large earnings losses, while the remaining workers gained. Policies that cushion the effects on displaced workers, such as active labour-market programmes targeted at older low-wage workers and the extension of unemployment insurance to workers with short contribution histories, are a natural complement to minimum-wage increases of this size.",
